{"status":"confirmed","reasoning":"Both earlier answers remain correct and agree with their respective captured worked solutions. For q-47367, the limit at x=1 is 1, whereas f(1)=2. The function is therefore discontinuous and not differentiable at 1, making I and II false. III is true in the standard sense that differentiability on an interval requires differentiability at every point of that interval; it does not mean the function is nowhere differentiable there. For q-73260, differentiability at 4 implies continuity at 4, so I is true. A corner at 2 prevents differentiability there, so II is true. Continuity at 0 alone does not guarantee differentiability, so III is not necessarily true. No graded submissions are supplied; MA evidence comes from each question's own worked solution.","confidence":1,"answers":[{"key":"q-47367/selection","correct_value":"III only","correct_option":"2","value_type":"text","wrong_value":null,"correct_keys":[],"wrong_keys":[],"ma_value":"III only","ma_basis":"worked_solution","ma_evidence":"Therefore, only statement III is true.","disagrees":false},{"key":"q-73260/selection","correct_value":"I and II only","correct_option":"1","value_type":"text","wrong_value":null,"correct_keys":[],"wrong_keys":[],"ma_value":"I and II only","ma_basis":"worked_solution","ma_evidence":"- Statement I is true. Differentiability implies continuity. Hence, ${lim}_{x\to 4}f(x)=f(4).$

- Statement II is true. The function has a corner point at $x=2$, so it's not differentiable there.

- Statement III is false.  Continuity does not imply differentiability. For instance, the function  $g(x)=|x|$ is continuous at $x=0$ but it is not differentiable there.","disagrees":false}]}
